WEBVTT

00:00:01.661 --> 00:00:07.176
This is Lecture Lab, Group sixty one. This recording is an automated browser
walkthrough with

00:00:07.176 --> 00:00:12.692
synthetic narration. The generation request is real, not a prewritten sample. The
public project page

00:00:12.692 --> 00:00:18.207
links to the running application, source code, and measured evaluation results.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:25.404
We open a fresh browser session and upload the project's own two page mathematics
example.

00:00:25.404 --> 00:00:30.588
It contains no student data. We request one multiple choice, one true or false,
and

00:00:30.588 --> 00:00:35.772
one short answer question. These are three independent quantity controls.

00:00:37.777 --> 00:00:43.043
Now we submit the real request to the configured DeepSeek service. The API key
stays

00:00:43.043 --> 00:00:48.308
on the host and is not shown to the browser. This continuous recording keeps the

00:00:48.308 --> 00:00:53.574
processing wait visible. Output can vary between runs, and service or quota
failures remain possible.

00:00:55.590 --> 00:01:01.075
The completed set is now visible. We check the actual question types and source
identities

00:01:01.075 --> 00:01:06.561
against the request. Generated mathematics is rendered with KaTeX. Structural
checks and matching quotations help

00:01:06.561 --> 00:01:12.046
traceability, but they do not prove that every answer or distractor is correct.

00:01:14.057 --> 00:01:18.605
For this demonstration, we deliberately choose an incorrect objective answer and
complete the other inputs.

00:01:18.605 --> 00:01:23.152
After submission, the wrong answer has a prominent red message, the selected
answer is shown,

00:01:23.152 --> 00:01:27.700
and the reference answer is highlighted. Short answers require self assessment;
this is not automatic

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:32.247
semantic grading.

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:40.039
Opening the evidence displays the actual original PDF passage, preserving its
mathematical glyphs and spacing.

00:01:40.039 --> 00:01:45.827
The raw extracted text remains available separately, because extraction can lose
symbols. We can compare

00:01:45.827 --> 00:01:51.616
the model explanation directly with the source rather than trusting a matching
citation alone.

00:01:53.621 --> 00:01:59.566
Next we refresh the page. The quiz, answers, and review state persist in the same

00:01:59.566 --> 00:02:05.512
browser and origin. Separate generated sets have separate history entries. This is
local browser storage,

00:02:05.512 --> 00:02:11.457
not cross device account synchronisation, and a changed hostname does not
automatically migrate these records.

00:02:13.475 --> 00:02:17.710
Here is an actual boundary case. We upload an image only scan of the same

00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:21.946
author written example. The parser rejects it with an instruction to use O C R

00:02:21.946 --> 00:02:26.181
first. This application does not claim built in O C R. The rejection occurs before

00:02:26.181 --> 00:02:30.416
any additional model generation request.

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:50.029
Finally, the public evaluation records real course material runs separately from
mocked interface tests. Revised

00:02:50.029 --> 00:02:56.307
runs retained twenty one of twenty one requested questions, versus twenty of
twenty one initially,

00:02:56.307 --> 00:03:02.586
in a small exploratory comparison. An ambiguous distractor remains documented.
There is no independent human

00:03:02.586 --> 00:03:08.864
accuracy estimate. The owner computer, network, tunnel, and API quota must remain
available.
